Experiment 8: Insertion Sort with Duplicate Elements
Aim

To sort an array that contains duplicate elements using Insertion Sort, and to show that equal elements keep their original order (the sort is stable).

Algorithm
Treat the first element as the sorted part.
Take the next element as key.
Compare key with the sorted part, moving from right to left.
Shift every element that is greater than key one position to the right. Equal elements are not shifted.
Put key in the empty position.
Repeat steps 2 to 5 until the last element is placed.
Pseudo code
InsertionSort(A, n)
  for i = 1 to n-1
      key = A[i]
      j = i - 1
      while j >= 0 and A[j] > key
          A[j+1] = A[j]
          j = j - 1
      A[j+1] = key
Source code (C)
c
#include <stdio.h>

void printArray(int a[], int n) {
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    printf("\n");
}

int main() {
    int a[8] = {5, 3, 8, 3, 5, 1, 8, 3};
    int n = 8, i, j, key;

    printf("Original array: ");
    printArray(a, n);

    for (i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        key = a[i];
        j = i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) {
            a[j + 1] = a[j];
            j--;
        }
        a[j + 1] = key;

        printf("After pass %d  : ", i);
        printArray(a, n);
    }

    printf("Sorted array  : ");
    printArray(a, n);
    return 0;
}
Output
Original array: 5 3 8 3 5 1 8 3 
After pass 1  : 3 5 8 3 5 1 8 3 
After pass 2  : 3 5 8 3 5 1 8 3 
After pass 3  : 3 3 5 8 5 1 8 3 
After pass 4  : 3 3 5 5 8 1 8 3 
After pass 5  : 1 3 3 5 5 8 8 3 
After pass 6  : 1 3 3 5 5 8 8 3 
After pass 7  : 1 3 3 3 5 5 8 8 
Sorted array  : 1 3 3 3 5 5 8 8
Result

Insertion Sort sorted the array with duplicates correctly. The shifting condition is a[j] > key (strictly greater), so equal elements are never moved past each other and the sort is stable.

Time complexity: Worst and average case O(n²); best case O(n) (already sorted array).
Space complexity: O(1).
